A shallow copy duplicates an object's top-level structure while copying references to any nested objects or arrays. If you change a nested object in the copy, it also changes the original.
A deep copy recursively duplicates the entire object hierarchy, creating a completely independent copy of every nested object and array. Changes to the deep copy never affect the original.
Shallow copy has Side Effects when mutating nested objects.
Shallow copy is faster and uses less memory.
